213:The first natural question about any DC-pair $P,Q$ (which can be made monic after applying  some simple  automorphism) is the following: what is the shape of a normal form of $Q$ with respect to $P$? We proved (step 1 of the proof of theorem \ref{T:DC-pairs} and lemma \ref{L:Q_p}) that this normal form has a very simple structure -- it is a sum of an operator from the centraliser $C(\partial^{\Ord (P)})\subset \hat{D}_1^{sym}$ of order $q=\Ord (Q)$ and a homogeneous operator of order $-p:=-\Ord (P)$, which is uniquely determined and whose shape can be explicitly calculated (let's call it the "tail"):
282: E_p=K^{\oplus p}[\Gamma_1]((\tilde{D}^{-1})) 
286:\tilde{D}^{-1}a=\sigma (a)\tilde{D}^{-1}, \quad a\in K^{\oplus p}[\Gamma_1] \quad \mbox{where \quad }
289:\sigma (a_0, \ldots ,a_{k-1})=(a_{k-1}, a_0, \ldots , a_{k-2}), \quad \sigma (\Gamma_1)=\Gamma_1-1 
