  2877	\begin{lemma}
  2878	\label{L:simple_automorphisms}
  2879	There exists an automorphism $\varphi_t\in \Aut(A_1)$ ($t\in K$): $\varphi_t(\partial)=\partial+c$, $\varphi_t (x)=x$, where $c\in K$ depends on $t$, such that 
  2880	$$
  2881	\varphi_t (\tilde{g})=\alpha^{l}y^d+ d\alpha^{l}ty^{d-1}+\ldots ,\quad \varphi_t (H_1)=\varepsilon \alpha^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}l/d +1}y^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}+1}+(\tilde{M}_{I-1}+1)\varepsilon \alpha^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}l/d +1}ty^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}}+\ldots
  2882	$$ 
  2883	where $\ldots$ means terms of lower degree.
  2884	\end{lemma}
  2885	
  2886	\begin{proof}
  2887	By theorem \ref{T:subrectangular_sequence} we have two possibilities: either $\tilde{g}$ has one root, say  $\tilde{g}=(b+\tilde{\alpha} y)^d$, or $\tilde{g}$ has more than one root. 
  2888	
  2889	In the first case $H_1=c(b+\tilde{\alpha} y)^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}+1}$, $c\in K$, and our claim is trivial. In the second case $d=ld_2$ and $H_1$ satisfies differential equation \eqref{E:dif_eq_H_1}. Obviously, there is an automorphism  $\varphi(\partial)=\partial+c$, $\varphi (x)=x$ such that $\varphi (\tilde{g})=\alpha^{l}y^d + c_{d-2}y^{d-2}+\ldots$. So, it is sufficient to show that $\varphi (H_1)$ also has zero coefficient at the second place (in this case we can find another automorphism which satisfies our claim). 
  2890	
  2891	So, assume $\tilde{g}=\alpha^{l}y^d + c_{d-2}y^{d-2}+\ldots$, $H_1=h_0y^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}+1}+h_1y^{\tilde{M}_{I-1}}+\ldots $. From theorem \ref{T:subrectangular_sequence} we know that $\tilde{M}_{I-1}=((l(M-n)+1)n_{I-1}-1)d_2$, and the degree of polynomial $H$ from equation \eqref{E:dif_eq_H_1} is $(l(M-n)+1)d_2$. Suppose  
  2892	$H=\bar{h}_0y^{(l(M-n)+1)d_2}+\bar{h}_1y^{(l(M-n)+1)d_2-1}+\ldots $. Then the equation on the coefficient $\bar{h}_1$ from equation \eqref{E:dif_eq_H} is (here we use that $d_3=l_3d_2$, as $d=ld_2$)
  2893	$$
  2894	\bar{h}_1((l(M-n)+1)d_2-1)\alpha^{l_3}-\bar{h}_1((l(M-n)+1)d_2\alpha^{l_3}=0,
  2895	$$
  2896	whence $\bar{h}_1=0$, and the equation on the coefficient $h_1$ from equation \eqref{E:dif_eq_H_1} is
  2897	$$
  2898	h_1(((l(M-n)+1)n_{I-1}-1)d_2)\alpha^{l_3}-h_1((l(M-n)+1)n_{I-1}d_2\alpha^{l_3}=0,
  2899	$$
  2900	whence $h_1=0$, and we are done.
  2901	\end{proof}
